Fyrclast

Ingredients: NORDIC JAZZ + PUNK ROCK + CINEMATIC, EPIC & ORCHESTRAL + Bass Guitar
Creative Catalyst: Its pioneers were lighthouse keepers broadcasting to ships through long polar nights.

Fyrclast is the violent collision of glacial ECM-style Nordic jazz, blistering crust-punk fury, and panoramic cinematic orchestration, tethered together by a weaponized, fuzz-saturated bass guitar. It sounds like an Arctic foghorn detonating inside a cathedral: breathy, melancholic brass melodies float over vast open chords of ice and silence, only to be pulverized without warning by 220-BPM punk d-beats, thundering orchestral timpani, and a subterranean bass guitar clanking like loose anchor chains against a rusted hull.

Genre DNA

- Tempo & Pulse: Wild, volatile swings from 0 BPM rubato stillness (suspended, glacial brass drones) into hyper-kinetic 190–235 BPM d-beats and double-time orchestral charges driven by aggressive downpicked bass.
- Harmonic Palette: Haunting Nordic folk modes (Dorian and Lydian blåtoner) and spacious minor ninths crashed violently against dissonant tritone power chords and Wagnerian orchestral brass clusters.
- Production Signatures: 
  1. Radio-Torn Gating: Giant hall reverbs and lush orchestral strings brutally slammed through overloaded transistor shortwave transmitters and harsh noise gates.
  2. Anchor-Clank Bass Rigging: Distorted Rickenbacker bass recorded through copper maritime megaphones and paired with sub-octave contra-bassoon lines for catastrophic low-end weight.
  3. Fresnel Modulation: Master-bus sidechain compression and tremolo calibrated precisely to the 6-second rotation cycle of a lighthouse beacon lamp.
- Dynamics: Extreme, heart-stopping polarity. A fragile, solitary trumpet sighing over bare bass harmonics is instantly obliterated by a zero-to-one-hundred blast of 40-piece orchestral brass, blastbeats, and screaming distortion.

Scene & Origin Lore

Fyrclast was born in the winter of 1979 across the automated-outpost transition on the Svalbard and Bjørnøya archipelagos. Trapped in total darkness for three straight months of mørketid (the Polar Night), eccentric lighthouse keepers and hydrographers fought isolation-induced madness (is-kollaps) by rigging their shortwave navigational transmitters into amateur pirate radio stations. Armed with pawn-shop bass guitars, battered trumpets, and stacks of symphony records left behind by past expeditions, these keepers hurled ferocious, cathartic sonic barricades across VHF Channel 16 to passing trawlers and icebreakers. The subculture spread along coastal Norway: dockworkers and punk squatter crews adopted the style, performing in salt-crusted yellow oilskins, grease-smeared wool sweaters, and brass navigational gauges worn as amulets, staging illegal live broadcasts during winter gales inside decommissioned coastal forts.

Central Instruments: Overdriven Rickenbacker Bass Guitars (strung with piano wire and run through blown marine radio heads), Nordic quarter-tone trumpet, pocket cornet, bowed concert timpani, 40-piece orchestral brass and string sections, maritime flare guns fired into resonant steel oil barrels, and harmoniums.

For Fans Of: Arve Henriksen, Refused, Colin Stetson, Swans

