Fictional EP Tracklist

Track 1: Diamond Dust Gouges the Mast
- Story & Context: In late November 1979 at the Bjørnøya outpost, Astrid Vinter heard the high-latitude blizzard vibrating the station's copper lightning rods into an unearthly orchestral scream. When an undocumented trawler drifted blind toward the breaker rocks, she cranked her fuzz-laden bass through the station's high-frequency emergency transmitter and hurled a 230-BPM punk charge over the airwaves to pierce their bridge cabin, guiding the panicked crew away from the shoals while screaming into the radio receiver: "Steer by the teeth of the horn, steer or split!" The desperate broadcast saved eleven lives and became the foundational holy text of Fyrclast.
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: Abstract cinematic album art showing a colossal wall of crystalline diamond dust ice particles shredding through the rigging of a fishing vessel in the Norwegian Sea, illuminated by the blinding red flash of an emergency flare, with fractured orchestral sheet music freezing into the waves.

Track 2: "Is That a Sun Dog or Gunfire?"
- Story & Context: Sixty days into the Polar Night, severe cabin fever prompted hornist Torstein Skau to mistake atmospheric refraction arcs in the freezing mist for Soviet gunboat muzzle flashes. Smelling of diesel fuel and rancid seal tallow, he wept into his trumpet in the lantern cupola, spinning a tragic, breathy Nordic jazz elegy that Astrid violently ruptured with clanking, overdriven bass lines and orchestral brass stabs. The pirate transmission provoked an actual Soviet sub commander to break radio silence solely to ask if the island was under bombardment, to which Torstein rasped the legendary confession: "We set fire to the prism just to see our bones!"
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: Surreal, claustrophobic oil painting of a lighthouse lantern room looking out at an optical sun dog halo in a blizzard, glowing brass lenses casting spectral halos across an interior littered with vacuum tubes, copper bass strings, and a single dented trumpet lying in melting snow.

Track 3: Null-Phase Aurora Over Sørkapp
- Story & Context: Long after the lighthouse was automated and the trio abandoned the island, Barents Sea mariners reported that during nights of intense solar storms, their navigational VHF radios would spontaneously light up with phantom audio. Captains running near Sørkapp still claim to hear the ghost resonance of hyper-fast d-beats, sweeping cinematic strings, and Astrid's sledgehammer bass clatter bleeding through the northern lights, punctuated by a disembodied crew shouting through the static: "The light keeps no graveyard for kings!" Scientists call it ionospheric scatter, but the fishing fleet treats the frequency as a sacred warning siren.
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: Epic, wide-angle cinematic shot of a spectral green and violet aurora borealis violently twisting over a black ocean and jagged sea-stacks, where radio wave vectors and orchestral sound waves take physical form as glowing, sharp glass shards plunging into the dark water.

